{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('state.json'))\ni=[x for x in st['interpretations'] if x['id']=='036a51af08b045e29165336ee017f032-interpretation-1'][0]\nprint(i['rationale'])\nprint('---LIMITS---')\nfor l in i['limitations']: print('*', l)\nprint('---CE---')\nfor c in i['counterevidence']: print('*', c)\nprint('---JH---')\nprint(i['joint_history'])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-17_17-50-22-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('state.json'))\ni=[x for x in st['interpretations'] if x['id']=='036a51af08b045e29165336ee017f032-interpretation-1'][0]\nprint(i['rationale'])\nprint('---LIMITS---')\nfor l in i['limitations']: print('*', l)\nprint('---CE---')\nfor c in i['counterevidence']: print('*', c)\nprint('---JH---')\nprint(i['joint_history'])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"Conclusion: violated, and the scoped defect is asserted. VerifyLeader reports success although the leader can reach fewer voters than the voter quorum it sized, because acknowledgements arriving on non-voter replication streams are counted as support. Evidence from receipt 7a54f3754b0b423aaf1ba6c0365367c0 (completed, tests_passed, exit 0). Parsed index 1, the trigger: five members were bootstrapped, two were demoted to non-voters, and the leader's transport was disconnected in both directions from the two remaining voters, so voters_reachable=1 against quorum_voters=2 while nonvoters_reachable=2; VerifyLeader completed with verify_ok=true and an empty error, and the node was still Leader. Parsed index 3, the control on an identically built cluster with the non-voters also disconnected (nonvoters_reachable=0): the same call completed with verify_ok=false and the error 'leadership lost while committing log', the node by then a Follower. The two cases differ only in whether the non-voters could answer, so the successful verification in the trigger is attributable to non-voter acknowledgements rather than to voter support. Mechanism from source: verifyLeader sets quorumSize with quorumSize(), which counts only voters (raft.go:1087-1100), but registers the verify future on every entry of leaderState.replState (raft.go:966-990), and startStopReplication creates a replication state for every configured server including non-voters (raft.go:583-600); any successful heartbeat or AppendEntries response calls notifyAll(true) (replication.go:100-112), which increments the verify future's vote count (future.go:274-293) and completes it at the voter-sized quorum. The library already uses the correct supporter set one function away: checkLeaderLease iterates the latest configuration and counts only voters (raft.go:1037-1050), so the two paths disagree about who can establish leadership. Consequence under the exercised conditions: a client that calls VerifyLeader to protect a read (api.go:927-931) is told the leader still holds a voter quorum while it can reach none of the other voters, so the guard against serving stale reads after leadership loss does not hold in a cluster that contains non-voters. A successor run, receipt e49121154f514e39b6c1a37a2c353a4a, repeated the call every 50 ms after the first verification and quantified the window: parsed index 1 again records verify_ok=true for the partitioned leader, and parsed index 2 records loop_successes=20 with ms_until_failure=1015 before the node became a Follower, so twenty separate verifications succeeded across about one second; the control at parsed index 4 recorded verify_ok=false and parsed index 5 recorded loop_successes=0 with failure at 0 ms. The false assurance therefore lasts for the leader lease interval (LeaderLeaseTimeout, one second in this harness), not for a single call. A clean-copy repeat of the same fixed experiment, receipt 13b7844febba4a6d80c7331818c943f6, reproduced the window: parsed index 1 verify_ok=true, parsed index 2 loop_successes=20 with ms_until_failure=1014, parsed index 4 verify_ok=false and parsed index 5 loop_successes=0. Both runs therefore agree on the count and on a window of about one second at this lease setting. The membership document defines a non-voter as 'a server that receives log entries but is not considered for elections or commitment purposes' (membership.md:11-12), which supports treating a non-voter's acknowledgement as ineligible support for a leadership verification sized as a voter quorum.\n---LIMITS---\n* One configuration shape was exercised: three voters and two non-voters, with the leader cut from both other voters. Other suffrage mixes, a single non-voter, or a non-voter whose heartbeat is the only one in flight were not tested.\n* The exposure window was measured only at one lease setting: with LeaderLeaseTimeout of one second the partitioned leader returned twenty successful verifications over about 1,015 ms and, in a clean-copy repeat, twenty over about 1,014 ms before stepping down, so the window tracks the configured lease but was not varied.\n* No client reads were issued, so the end-to-end stale-read consequence is inferred from the documented purpose of VerifyLeader rather than observed.\n* The demotions completed before the partition, but each member's view of the new configuration was not read back before the transport was cut.\n* An attempt to observe the end-to-end stale read (majority commits a newer value while the partitioned leader verifies) did not succeed in construction: once the leader is cut from the voters it steps down at its next lease check, and forcing the majority side to campaign immediately still left the partitioned node a Follower by the time the new value had committed, so a positive verification concurrent with an already-stale FSM was not captured. Whether the two windows can be aligned in practice, and how wide the intersection is, remains unsettled.\n---CE---\n* The verification returned before the lease check stepped the leader down, so the false success is bounded by the leader lease interval rather than permanent; in the control that same step-down produced the error the trigger avoided.\n* VerifyLeader returning nil installed no leader and returned no data; the harm requires a client to rely on it as the guard the documentation describes, and this harness issued no reads.\n* Non-voter reachability does show the leader is alive and connected, so a deployment that used VerifyLeader only as a liveness probe would not be affected; the code sizes a voter quorum, which makes quorum the stated intent.\n* The partition was modelled by removing in-memory transport routing in both directions between the leader and the voters, not by dropping packets or crashing members.\n---JH---\nReceipt e49121154f514e39b6c1a37a2c353a4a is the successor of 533b966489544f45b4fcf14f6edce57f-experiment under this question: identical cases, admissions and first-verification assertions, with an added repeat loop. The two receipts agree on the decisive outcomes (trigger succeeds, control fails), so they describe the same subject; the successor adds the exposure measurement the earlier receipt lacked. Receipt 13b7844febba4a6d80c7331818c943f6 is a clean-copy repeat of the successor with the same saved inputs; it adds reproducibility of the window measurement and introduces no different basis.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
